  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1104 AM MST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nDRY AND MILD THIS AFTERNOON:\n\nMid and high clouds continue to spill in from the northwest as \na strong atmospheric river carried by a low pressure trough \npushes into the Pacific Northwest as seen on satellite imagery. \nThis moisture is being carried up and over a high pressure ridge\nsitting over the Desert Southwest but is showing signs of \nbreaking down. Conditions this afternoon will remain dry with \nshowers increasing this evening over the northern mountains as \nthis moisture moves into the northern portion of our CWA. \nAnticipate mild temperatures about 5 degrees above normal this \nafternoon.\n\nMOUNTAIN SNOWFALL TONIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY NIGHT:\n\nA strong 140 kt jet will accompany this rather robust shortwave\nas it tracks through the Intermountain West and Northern \nRockies with the jet setting up tonight over northern Utah into \nsouthwest Colorado, with the northern and central Colorado \nmountains in the more favored area for strong lift. Specific \nhumidity climbs above 4 g/kg with PWAT anomalies above 200 \npercent of normal across the north so even though we are on the \ntail end of this AR moisture as it moves further inland, we will\nhave plenty to work with. The strongest period of forcing looks\nto occur Saturday morning into the early afternoon, with the \nhighest snowfall rates expected between 5 am and 5 pm Saturday. \nThis is a favorable setup for strong northwest orographics in \naddition to the dynamics and QG forcing during this time. Post- \nThanksgiving travel will be impacted during the morning and \nevening commutes on Saturday so be prepared for difficult winter\ndriving conditions with blowing snow also contributing to \nreduced visibilities as winds are expected to gust 40 to 50 mph \nover the higher terrain and passes. The lower elevations could \nsee winds gusting 30 to 40 mph as well at times but look to \nremain rain if anything. The impacts will be in the northern and\ncentral mountains with the Park Range on track to receive 10 to\n15 inches. Probabilities of over a foot of snow is highest in \nthe Park Range with chances 70 to 80 percent, so the Winter \nStorm Warning remains on track with Winter Weather Advisories \nfor the Flattops and Gore and Elk Mountains including the I-70 \nmountain corridor through Vail and Vail Pass where 4 to 8 inches\nof snow is expected. Timing of these winter highlights is 5 pm \ntoday through 5 am Sunday as snow looks to continue through \nearly Sunday morning.\n\nSUNDAY AND BEYOND:\n\nA transitory ridge will slide in for Sunday and Monday with \ndrier conditions expected. Some high clouds will continue to \npass overhead due to another system moving into the Pacific \nNorthwest but this one doesn't appear to impact us much as it \ntracks to our northwest as it moves inland and weakens. The next\npotential weather maker to watch for is another AR and low \npressure trough that moves onto the west coast Tuesday and \npotentially brings more mountain snowfall to our region during \nthe mid week timeframe. There is still quite a bit of model \nuncertainty with this system with differences in timing and \nstorm track. The GFS takes a more southerly track with this low \nbecoming closed off and splitting as it tracks south of the Four\nCorners through Arizona and New Mexico Wednesday evening \nthrough Thursday. The ECMWF track is a bit further north coming \nin as an open wave trough but still favoring the southern \nmountains. Model guidance remains in relatively poor agreement, \nwith significant spread. The southerly track does increase \npotential for atmospheric river moisture to accompany this \nsystem. However, the exact track of this system can make the \ndifference between a solid mountain snowfall and seeing little \nto no snow at all. We shall have to wait and see how the \nforecast for this system develops over the coming days.",
